  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nSubtle upper-level ridging remains over the area on Wednesday. This \nwill promote warmer than normal high temperatures ranging from the \nlow to upper 80s for most locations. By Wednesday night, long-range \nguidance has an inverted upper trough developing across portions of \nTexas extending into western New Mexico. A disturbance in the \nsouthwest Gulf along with southeasterly to easterly winds bring \nsurface moisture to the region. As a result, slightly cooler weather \nand low to medium (10-40%) rain chances are in store Thursday \nthrough Saturday. These chances are forecast mainly over portions \nof the Lower Trans Pecos and Big Bend region each afternoon due to\ndaytime heating and upslope flow acting as additional mechanisms \nfor shower/storm development. PWATs are forecast to range from the\n75th to near 90th climatological percentile indicating the \npotential for heavy rainfall. Given the mainly isolated to \nscattered coverage, the flash flood threat remains very low at the\nmoment. Surface high pressure from the Rockies will keep dry air \nin the lower levels for areas north of the aforementioned areas, \nresulting in very low rain/storm chances (~10%). Highs each \nafternoon are expected in the mid 70s to low 80s for most. A \nwarming and drying trend commences late this weekend into early \nnext week as upper-level ridging returns. Guidance has high \ntemperatures rebounding back into the low to upper 80s with some \nlocations along and near the river valleys reaching into the low \n90s. \n\nLamberson",
